  "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t)=-3x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{\u2032}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,-1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,0]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$",
